{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1788139124,"nonce":"/WFMywPmlNWSkGiQpphnykkuKnY=","target":"1d9c3b345ad0e61d904bbf87cbc972f90a602cc7b5c34f37d4a5d318a6507276","message":"OPEN-QUESTION RESOLUTIONS — 2.4 worker, branch `spec/2.4-world-serializers`.\nAll three resolved inside this spec; none of them changes another spec's contract.\n\n(Reposted: `git-bug bug comment new -F` silently DROPS every line beginning with\n`#`, so the first version of this comment lost all six of its markdown headings.\nREADME's trap list should gain that — it names `-F` for apostrophes but not for\nthis. No heading in this comment starts with `#`.)\n\n**1. Category taxonomy for `things`**\n\nRESOLVED: the taxonomy is the game's, not ours. Twelve of fourteen words map to\na `ThingRequestGroup`; the other two name a `ThingDef` property.\n\n    food      -\u003e group FoodSourceNotPlantOrTree   (the word 2.3's `nearest` already uses)\n    meds      -\u003e group Medicine                   (ditto)\n    apparel   -\u003e group Apparel                    (ditto)\n    weapons   -\u003e group Weapon                     (ditto)\n    drugs     -\u003e group Drug\n    corpses   -\u003e group Corpse\n    chunks    -\u003e group Chunk\n    art       -\u003e group Art\n    plants    -\u003e group Plant\n    beds      -\u003e group Bed\n    buildings -\u003e group BuildingArtificial\n    haulable  -\u003e group HaulableEver               (the default when nothing is asked)\n    all       -\u003e group Everything\n    resources -\u003e group HaulableEver + ThingDef.CountAsResource\n\nRationale, three parts:\n\n- **A group is a membership list the game already maintains.** `ListerThings`\n  keeps one list per group and fills it in `Add()` from\n  `ThingListGroupHelper.Includes`. Reading one is a list read; inventing a\n  bucket would be a predicate over every thing on the map, and it would drift\n  from the game's own answer the first time a DLC or a bench mod adds a def.\n- **The four words 2.3 already shipped keep their meaning.** `nearest\n  --category food|meds|apparel|weapons` resolves to exactly these four groups.\n  Two verbs answering \"food\" differently would be the worse defect.\n- **`resources` has no group, so it borrows the resource readout's own\n  definition.** `ThingDef.CountAsResource` is `resourceReadoutPriority !=\n  Uncounted` — literally what the top-left readout counts, which is what a\n  player means by \"resources\".\n\nEvery result publishes `query.category_source` (e.g. `\"group:Apparel\"`,\n`\"group:HaulableEver + ThingDef.CountAsResource\"`), so a consumer reads the\ndefinition instead of guessing it, and a future word cannot quietly redefine an\nold one.\n\n**2. How filter summaries compress (storage filters are trees)**\n\nRESOLVED: a bounded category-tree walk over the real `allowedDefs` set, with the\ndenominator always named. Neither of the two obvious routes may be used, and\nboth for reasons worth recording.\n\n- `ThingFilter.Summary` is read-only and USELESS. Its first two branches need\n  the XML-only `thingDefs` / `categories` lists, which a runtime storage filter\n  does not have, and its third needs exactly one allowed def. A real stockpile\n  filter falls all the way through to the literal `\"UsableIngredients\"`\n  translation — the same four words for every stockpile on the map.\n- `ThingFilter.DisplayRootCategory` — the field the storage tab actually draws\n  from — is a LAZY-INIT GETTER that runs a `GenThreading.ParallelFor` over every\n  ThingCategory node crossed with every allowed def, and whose setter then runs\n  `RecalculateSpecialFilterConfigurability()`, writing\n  `allowedHitPointsConfigurable` and `allowedQualitiesConfigurable`. An observer\n  must not be the thing that runs it.\n\nSo `FilterSummary.Build(filter, universe, universeName)`:\n\n- one bottom-up pass credits each def's categories to the root, once per node\n  per def (a def in two categories sharing an ancestor is not double-counted),\n  building `allowed` from `filter.AllowedThingDefs` (the real HashSet) and\n  `total` from the universe;\n- the walk then descends from `ThingCategoryDefOf.Root` ONLY into nodes that are\n  partially allowed. A node that is all-in or all-out is one line and its whole\n  subtree is dropped — that is the compression. Bounded by `NodeCap = 24`,\n  `DepthCap = 4`, with `categories_more` publishing what the cap cost;\n- a filter with \u003c= 12 allowed defs also gets `defs: [...]` verbatim, because\n  \"steel, plasteel\" beats \"ResourcesRaw 2/38\";\n- `hp_range_pct` and `quality_range` come from the plain field getters and\n  appear only when they are not the full range.\n\nTHE DENOMINATOR IS ALWAYS NAMED in `universe`, because \"8/12\" is uninterpretable\nwithout it: a stockpile passes its own `GetParentStoreSettings().filter` (the\nEverStorable fixed filter — exactly what its tab could offer), a bill passes\n`recipe.fixedIngredientFilter`, and an outfit/food policy has no natural parent\nso it falls back to every ThingDef in the category\n(`universe:\"all-thingdefs\"`, computed once per session).\n\n**3. What an \"opaque dialog\" reports for an unknown modded window**\n\nRESOLVED: a three-tier ladder under ONE window vocabulary — 1.7's — with the\nopaque tier scraping FIELDS ONLY, never properties.\n\nThe head of every window entry is 1.7's four fields verbatim (`type`,\n`type_full`, `title?`, `layer`) plus `force_pause` and `absorbs_input`; `kind`\nsays which tier answered. The force-pause half of the result is\n`TimeDriver.ForcePausePayload(stack)`'s output unmodified under `force_pause`,\nso rwtest asserts on one shape.\n\n1. `node-tree` — `Dialog_NodeTree` and every subclass, which is what EVERY\n   ChoiceLetter opens (`ChoiceLetter.OpenLetter` builds a DiaNode from `Choices`\n   and stacks a `Dialog_NodeTreeWithFactionInfo`). `options[]` is read off the\n   current node: `{index, label, disabled, disabled_reason, closes, opens_node}`.\n2. `message-box` — `Dialog_MessageBox`, three public button-text fields. Mods\n   reach for it constantly (the charity confirmation inside\n   `ChoiceLetter_AcceptVisitors` is vanilla doing exactly that).\n3. `opaque` — everything else. The window is scraped for candidate text:\n   instance fields only, walking the type chain up to `Window`, collecting\n   strings, TaggedStrings and the labels of any `List\u003cDiaOption\u003e` or\n   `List\u003cFloatMenuOption\u003e` it holds. Capped at 8 values, 160 chars each.\n\nFIELDS, NEVER PROPERTIES, and that is the load-bearing part of the resolution. A\nfield read cannot execute game code. A property getter on a window class we have\nnever seen is arbitrary code, and this project has now found lazy-init getters\nthat write the save in `Pawn_OutfitTracker`, `Zone_Growing`, `ResearchManager`\nand `Bill_Production` — reflecting over properties on Hospitality's,\nGastronomy's, Storefront's and Guests' windows would be inviting that class of\nbug from a mod we cannot audit. The restriction is what makes tier 3 safe to run\nsight unseen, which is what the session-4 amendment asked for.\n\nNothing in `interactions` closes, focuses, reorders or answers a window.\nDeciding what a dialog MEANS stays 3.5's.\n\n**Also settled, though not asked**\n\nChoiceLetter option labels need reflection. `DiaOption.text` is\n`protected string` with no public accessor at all (`Verse/DiaOption.cs`), so the\nwords on the button come from an `AccessTools` field ref, bound once and\ntolerantly, with `source: \"backing-field\" | \"unavailable\"` published beside the\ndata — PawnSafe's discipline. `interactions` reports `label: null` with\n`source:\"unavailable\"` rather than an empty string, so \"we could not look\" and\n\"the button has no text\" stay distinguishable.","files":null}]}